  "title": "Environment-variable scope: the value a child process SEES, not the value that was written",
  "question": "Measured on this estate: `setx` writes the persistent store but misses the already-running session, so a tool reads the OLD value while the writer believes it succeeded - and the same class re-appeared as a config key written at the wrong SCOPE (root instead of per-project) that was inert while looking correct. Target: every test that depends on an environment variable or a scoped config key asserts the value OBSERVED BY THE CHILD PROCESS that consumes it, never the value the setter wrote. Which scope does each consumer actually read? (Shell-script line endings are owned by TST-0192 in slice TST-2.)",
  "sentinel": "SUCCESS:TST-0067",
